ACTIVITY6 = '''<article class="activity" id="choose-part">
<p class="eyebrow">06 / Production choices &amp; collaboration · about 5 minutes</p>
<h2>Choose a part. Move it around.</h2>
<p>Production includes decisions about what returns and where it lands. Try a small arrangement using material you make for this exercise. Write, speak privately, quietly tap, or imagine it. No hearing task or recording is required.</p>
<div class="example"><span>Four original teaching phrases</span><p class="verse">Doors open.<br>Lights low.<br>Ready now.<br>Take a breath.</p></div>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Make four parts.</strong> Invent four short phrases, patterns or descriptions of sounds. You can use our phrases instead.</li><li><strong>Choose one part.</strong> What do you want someone to notice? Repeat that part once.</li><li><strong>Change the placement.</strong> Try the repeat earlier or later, or put a pause before it. Keep two versions on paper, in your notes or in memory.</li><li><strong>Explain the difference.</strong> What changes in the meaning, pattern or feeling? Choose a version, combine them, or decide the repeat does not help.</li></ol>
<details><summary>Compare two possible arrangements</summary><p><strong>Version A:</strong> Doors open. Lights low. Ready now. Ready now. Take a breath.</p><p><strong>Version B:</strong> Doors open. Lights low. Ready now. Take a breath. [pause] Ready now.</p><p>A could make readiness feel insistent. B could make the last phrase feel like a decision after a breath. You may read them differently. Neither arrangement is the correct one.</p></details>
<div class="note"><strong>A collaborator is optional</strong><p>Ask a willing person what they notice and invite one alternative. Explain your goal before comparing. You can keep your first choice; feedback is a suggestion. A solo or silent response works too.</p></div>
<p class="source"><a href="stories.html#badu-dilla">Badu’s account of a lesson with J Dilla</a> offers one collaboration example. These phrases and this exercise are ours, not their music or method.</p>
<h3>One learning connection</h3><p>Compare repetition or a pause in a speech, reading or group project with a teacher or mentor. Practice explaining a choice and asking a clear feedback question.</p>
<a class="button secondary" href="next-step.html#collaboration">Find help with a creative choice</a></article>'''

